🧠 How the AI Should Work

For example, a user enters:

Plan a traditional wedding in Andhra Pradesh for 300 guests with a budget of ₹12 lakh. Include catering, decorations, photography, and a six-month timeline.

EventEase AI should then produce:

Personalized concept: A traditional wedding with a coordinated color palette and decoration suggestions.
Budget plan: Estimated spending across the venue, catering, photography, decorations, and other categories.
Timeline: Tasks organized by deadlines leading up to the wedding.
Vendor checklist: Services to research, compare, and book.
Savings suggestions: Practical alternatives if the estimated spending exceeds the budget.
Event-day schedule: A coordinated checklist for the organizers.

All estimates should be clearly labeled, and actual financial calculations should be performed by application code rather than left to the AI model.

🛠️ Recommended Development Approach

To make the app impressive while keeping it practical:

Build the complete visual interface first. Include the landing page, dashboard, and all primary screens.
Make demo mode fully functional. Users should be able to explore the app without an API key.
Implement the core planning engine. Event creation, budgets, tasks, and vendor checklists should work independently of the AI.
Connect Gemini through a secure server-side API. Generate structured plans based on the user's requirements.
Test and deploy. Run the checks, fix errors, and prepare the project for deployment on Vercel.

My recommendation: Build EventEase AI as a polished, mobile-first web application with a working demo, personalized AI planning, accurate budget calculations, and visually rich event inspiration. That gives you both an attractive presentation and a useful application that people can actually try.